1
00:00:03,561 --> 00:00:07,204
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have several suttas about energy and effort.

2
00:00:07,744 --> 00:00:12,628
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a kind of general discussion about that and what I mean when the Buddha says that.

3
00:00:15,590 --> 00:00:21,895
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first thing I'll read is from Sanyutta Nikaya, the Bhojanga Sanyutta, the third sutta.

4
00:00:22,836 --> 00:00:24,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the enlightenment factors.

5
00:00:26,198 --> 00:00:40,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Says, starts off, bhikkhus, those bhikkhus who are accomplished in virtue, accomplished in composure, accomplished in wisdom, accomplished in liberation, accomplished in knowledge and vision of liberation, even the sight of those bhikkhus is helpful.

6
00:00:40,467 --> 00:00:43,209
[SPEAKER_01]: I say, even listening to them, even approaching them,

7
00:00:43,668 --> 00:00:48,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Even attending on them, even recollecting them, even going forth after them is helpful.

8
00:00:49,174 --> 00:00:50,515
[SPEAKER_01]: I say, for what reason?

9
00:00:50,935 --> 00:00:59,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when one has heard the dhamma from such a bhikkhu, one dwells withdrawn by two... one of two kinds of... by way of two kinds of withdrawal.

10
00:01:00,164 --> 00:01:02,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Withdrawal of the body and withdrawal of the mind.

11
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:09,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Dwelling thus withdrawn, one recollects the dhamma and thinks it over.

12
00:01:09,844 --> 00:01:13,827
[SPEAKER_01]: On that occasion, the enlightenment factor of mindfulness is aroused by the bhikkhu.

13
00:01:14,447 --> 00:01:17,409
[SPEAKER_01]: On that occasion, the bhikkhu develops the enlightenment factor of mindfulness.

14
00:01:18,049 --> 00:01:22,312
[SPEAKER_01]: On that occasion, the enlightenment factor of mindfulness comes to fulfillment by development in that bhikkhu.

15
00:01:24,274 --> 00:01:30,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Dwelling thus mindfully, he discriminates that dhamma with wisdom, examines it, makes an investigation of it.

16
00:01:30,918 --> 00:01:40,266
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever he does that, he's mindfully developing the discrimination of states or investigation factor.

17
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:47,171
[SPEAKER_01]: While he discriminates that dhamma with wisdom and examines it, his energy is aroused without slackening.

18
00:01:48,052 --> 00:01:57,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever a bhikkhu's energy is aroused without slackening, as he investigates the dhamma with wisdom and examines it, on that occasion, the enlightenment factor of energy is aroused by the bhikkhu.

19
00:01:59,208 --> 00:02:08,574
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, after that, it says, eh... when his energy is aroused, there arises in him spirit, eh... epīti, eh... translated rapture, usually.

20
00:02:09,115 --> 00:02:18,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever bhikkhu's rapture arises in bhikkhu, energy... his energy is aroused, on that occasion, eh... this alignment factor of rapture is aroused.

21
00:02:18,721 --> 00:02:19,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eh...

22
00:02:19,262 --> 00:02:19,442
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll...

23
00:02:19,502 --> 00:02:20,983
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll stop after this one for a bit.

24
00:02:21,623 --> 00:02:27,868
[SPEAKER_01]: For one whose mind is uplifted by rapture, the body becomes tranquil and the mind becomes tranquil.

25
00:02:35,617 --> 00:02:47,450
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... it sounds very like, you know, at first glance, you kind of think of energy as always this kind of, eh...

26
00:02:48,257 --> 00:02:49,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Like kind of over-energized.

27
00:02:49,938 --> 00:02:51,979
[SPEAKER_01]: That seems to be the usual kind of assumption.

28
00:02:52,539 --> 00:02:58,603
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's here saying that it's clearly within kind of the framework of... well, it's also for... what's the sake of that applying energy?

29
00:02:58,683 --> 00:03:01,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's for having a certain kind of tranquility.

30
00:03:02,145 --> 00:03:05,627
[SPEAKER_01]: But then people overthink, overdo tranquility too.

31
00:03:07,288 --> 00:03:12,651
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... as far as that goes, eh... eh...

32
00:03:14,113 --> 00:03:25,857
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that... what exactly is that kind of energy that allows for one to be tranquil and not to be like that kind of assumption that we have of just like too much?

33
00:03:28,758 --> 00:03:33,900
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's not... it's not an inspiration to do something, which is usually requires too much.

34
00:03:35,481 --> 00:03:38,582
[SPEAKER_02]: Requires you to be externally inspired, so to speak.

35
00:03:38,882 --> 00:03:41,423
[SPEAKER_02]: Find external reasons that will motivate you to do things.

36
00:03:42,470 --> 00:03:49,674
[SPEAKER_02]: It's more like the recognition from the... well, the recognition of the right view, first of all.

37
00:03:50,195 --> 00:04:04,223
[SPEAKER_02]: Then... because, see, the work that you need to do, in terms of freeing yourself from suffering, is nowhere else, except within your presently enduring situation, whatever that is.

38
00:04:05,084 --> 00:04:07,445
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not in the future, it's not in the past.

39
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:10,240
[SPEAKER_02]: It's right here.

40
00:04:10,360 --> 00:04:17,983
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're not free from suffering right here, right now, it's because right here, right now, you're still entertaining craving, or the view of craving.

41
00:04:18,903 --> 00:04:28,966
[SPEAKER_02]: So, once you actually discern that that work is not just that it's not elsewhere, you also discern that it's very doable.

42
00:04:29,026 --> 00:04:36,268
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's the recognition of that doability of the things you still need to do, the ones that still hasn't fulfilled the goal of Arahantship,

43
00:04:37,289 --> 00:04:38,610
[SPEAKER_02]: Naturally gives the energy to do it.

44
00:04:39,451 --> 00:04:49,800
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, you know, like, if you have something that you need to do, that's really, like, it requires so much preparation, so far away in the future, it's hard to get motivated to do it.

45
00:04:50,681 --> 00:04:58,808
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you recognize that doing this is literally only on you and up to you and within you, it doesn't require anything, anyone outside.

46
00:04:59,109 --> 00:05:00,330
[SPEAKER_02]: Same with tranquility as well.

47
00:05:01,745 --> 00:05:09,031
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not practicing tranquility in order to be... not being bothered by this person or by that person.

48
00:05:09,171 --> 00:05:12,134
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a natural calmness, because you realize it's entirely up to you.

49
00:05:13,635 --> 00:05:27,627
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, of course, you would want to develop it, because it's obviously very agreeable.

50
00:05:27,667 --> 00:05:29,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it sounds to me also like it's...

51
00:05:31,246 --> 00:05:37,453
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like something that... it's not like... it has to be almost like a steady kind of thing.

52
00:05:37,473 --> 00:05:39,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I'll read the other sutta I was going to mention.

53
00:05:40,436 --> 00:05:41,638
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the one that's quite...

54
00:06:10,548 --> 00:06:15,140
[SPEAKER_01]: This is in the Anguttara book of sixes, number 55, Bhikkhu Sona.

55
00:06:18,878 --> 00:06:23,222
[SPEAKER_01]: While the Venerable Sona was alone in seclusion, the following course of thought arose in his mind.

56
00:06:24,003 --> 00:06:29,929
[SPEAKER_01]: I am one of the Blessed One's most energetic disciples, yet my mind has not been liberated from the taints by non-singing.

57
00:06:30,589 --> 00:06:35,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, there is wealth in my family, and it is possible for me to enjoy my wealth and do meritorious deeds.

58
00:06:36,375 --> 00:06:41,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me then give up the training and return to lower life, so that I can enjoy my wealth and do meritorious deeds.

59
00:06:43,363 --> 00:06:50,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha, having known this course of thought in Sona's mind, went and appeared to him, and asked him if he was thinking that.

60
00:06:52,265 --> 00:06:54,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Sona says, yes, Bhante.

61
00:06:54,746 --> 00:07:01,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the Buddha asks, tell me, Sona, in the past, when you lived at home, weren't you skilled at the lute?

62
00:07:02,588 --> 00:07:03,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, Bhante.

63
00:07:04,028 --> 00:07:05,008
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think, Sona?

64
00:07:05,449 --> 00:07:09,350
[SPEAKER_01]: When its strings were too tight, was your lute well-tuned and easy to play?

65
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:10,870
[SPEAKER_01]: No, Bhante.

66
00:07:11,750 --> 00:07:14,872
[SPEAKER_01]: When its strings were too loose, was your lute well-tuned and easy to play?

67
00:07:15,572 --> 00:07:16,152
[SPEAKER_01]: No, Bhante.

68
00:07:16,913 --> 00:07:23,836
[SPEAKER_01]: But, Sona, when its strings were neither too tight nor too loose, but adjusted to a balanced pitch, was your lute well-tuned and easy to play?

69
00:07:24,476 --> 00:07:25,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, Bhante.

70
00:07:26,037 --> 00:07:31,080
[SPEAKER_01]: So, too, Sona, if energy is aroused too forcefully, this leads to restlessness.

71
00:07:31,780 --> 00:07:34,702
[SPEAKER_01]: And if energy is too lax, this leads to laziness.

72
00:07:35,403 --> 00:07:38,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, Sona, resolve on a balance of energy.

73
00:07:38,665 --> 00:07:43,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Achieve evenness of spiritual faculties and take... pick up your sign there.

74
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:47,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Pick up the sign of that, you might translate it.

75
00:07:47,432 --> 00:07:47,632
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

76
00:07:50,134 --> 00:08:00,982
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you... if you get the energy to do the work, if you lose the context, if you lose perspective on account of it, you'll become restless.

77
00:08:02,820 --> 00:08:03,761
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't, you won't.

78
00:08:05,682 --> 00:08:13,145
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he's so keen to uproot the craving, that he gave in to craving for uprooting it.

79
00:08:15,927 --> 00:08:23,191
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, that the energy to do it, by losing the right context in regard to it, he turned it into craving.

80
00:08:23,391 --> 00:08:24,431
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it became restlessness.

81
00:08:27,333 --> 00:08:30,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Or same, the energy to become... the intention to become,

82
00:08:32,892 --> 00:08:40,836
[SPEAKER_02]: If you lose the context of it, it becomes just lazy, careless, drowsy, sleepy, whatever else.

83
00:08:42,237 --> 00:08:44,599
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you keep the context, then it's just calm energy.

84
00:08:46,059 --> 00:08:49,561
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's what means they're getting... maintaining the balance means maintaining the context.

85
00:08:51,042 --> 00:08:55,725
[SPEAKER_02]: As to the... like... what the purpose of energy is, what the purpose of tranquility is.

86
00:08:58,518 --> 00:09:01,060
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, in order to do that, it's a matter of kind of...

87
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:02,641
[SPEAKER_02]: Maintaining mindfulness in regard to it.

88
00:09:04,843 --> 00:09:06,384
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you can't have too much of mindfulness.

89
00:09:09,986 --> 00:09:19,193
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, in a sense, it sounds as though, like, while you're doing whatever efforts you're doing, it's almost like... good to, like, really kind of...

90
00:09:19,453 --> 00:09:20,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, I'm thinking...

91
00:09:20,494 --> 00:09:21,534
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm going this route.

92
00:09:21,875 --> 00:09:24,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Why don't I check on the perspective of things now?

93
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:28,582
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's not... it's not up to you to decide.

94
00:09:29,502 --> 00:09:31,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I need energy now.

95
00:09:31,744 --> 00:09:35,546
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can discern that, sure, if that's what the situation dictates.

96
00:09:36,126 --> 00:09:38,167
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can't go, oh, let me just develop some energy.

97
00:09:39,228 --> 00:09:45,412
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to recognize, first, of course, the mindful context, as in, what is the purpose of all of this?

98
00:09:45,492 --> 00:09:48,333
[SPEAKER_02]: It's freedom from craving, always.

99
00:09:48,373 --> 00:09:50,615
[SPEAKER_02]: It never, never, never steers away from that purpose.

100
00:09:51,815 --> 00:09:54,237
[SPEAKER_02]: So, do you feel

101
00:09:55,637 --> 00:09:58,959
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, clear about the direction of freedom from craving?

102
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:03,442
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're clear enough, in a way, then okay.

103
00:10:03,462 --> 00:10:08,345
[SPEAKER_02]: So, keep the... keep the context in your mind, in a sense, of the purposes to be free from craving.

104
00:10:08,785 --> 00:10:10,906
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I need to understand craving.

105
00:10:10,926 --> 00:10:18,931
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can I make the effort to understand the craving without needing to crave for that effort, without needing to wind myself up in order to do it now?

106
00:10:20,132 --> 00:10:23,414
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can I get the energy without allowing that energy to make me restless?

107
00:10:24,750 --> 00:10:30,172
[SPEAKER_02]: By, again, not losing the right order of mindfulness, not losing the sight of mindfulness.

108
00:10:31,673 --> 00:10:32,993
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter.

109
00:10:33,013 --> 00:10:39,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you find out that it's more important to not act out of craving, even if you haven't understood craving yet.

110
00:10:40,497 --> 00:10:48,600
[SPEAKER_02]: It's when you forget that that's more important, you then choose to make more important, let me just act out of this craving, and then I'll figure it out, and then I'll never crave again.

111
00:10:50,136 --> 00:10:56,181
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you end up just revolving in a vicious circle, because you yourself maintain the craving in order to understand the way out of craving.

112
00:10:57,362 --> 00:11:07,630
[SPEAKER_02]: But the way out of craving is by cessation of craving, not by understanding it on your own terms.

113
00:11:07,650 --> 00:11:11,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you believe, then, say, you want to contemplate something and you don't want to,

114
00:11:12,428 --> 00:11:17,009
[SPEAKER_01]: If you feel muddled about the intention behind that... You need to know the intention behind, yeah.

115
00:11:17,129 --> 00:11:18,029
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I want to contemplate this.

116
00:11:18,169 --> 00:11:18,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

117
00:11:18,609 --> 00:11:22,210
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it because I want to get rid of the unpleasantness of craving?

118
00:11:23,190 --> 00:11:24,270
[SPEAKER_02]: Means you're acting out of craving.

119
00:11:25,831 --> 00:11:26,391
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, no, I don't...

120
00:11:26,411 --> 00:11:27,271
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't feel anything unpleasant.

121
00:11:27,291 --> 00:11:35,153
[SPEAKER_02]: I just want to invest some effort into, like, you know, steering a few things up to, you know, to create a work, because the mind was a bit dull.

122
00:11:36,153 --> 00:11:36,713
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's fine.

123
00:11:38,372 --> 00:11:40,916
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if your mind is not dull, then it's just too tight.

124
00:11:41,516 --> 00:11:44,501
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you actually want to be... No, I will not lose perspective.

125
00:11:45,122 --> 00:11:50,549
[SPEAKER_02]: See, not acting out of craving does not automatically mean, oh, I'll just go and sleep and be lazy and drowsy.

126
00:11:51,875 --> 00:11:52,295
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't.

127
00:11:52,335 --> 00:12:00,883
[SPEAKER_02]: It just means you don't lose the mindfulness, you don't sacrifice the priority of mindful context on account of what you want to do, even if it's to understand craving.

128
00:12:01,624 --> 00:12:11,012
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're not sure why you're trying to understand the craving, as in, is this rooted in trying to get rid of the suffering, or is it maybe root means, your doubtful means, you should calm yourself first.

129
00:12:11,533 --> 00:12:18,419
[SPEAKER_02]: And you calm yourself first by maintaining absence of craving is more important than acting out of craving even for the sake of getting rid of the craving.

130
00:12:20,181 --> 00:12:25,565
[SPEAKER_02]: And you are responsible for absence of craving or for presence of craving, because craving needs action to be maintained.

131
00:12:26,566 --> 00:12:27,526
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to act out of it.

132
00:12:28,047 --> 00:12:31,529
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're not acting out of it, you realize, okay, that's all I need to do, for now.

133
00:12:32,430 --> 00:12:42,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, when there is no pressure of craving, then the mind can maybe think, if it wants to still think about these things, but often you realize, the only reason you want to understand craving was just to get rid of the pain that was currently felt.

134
00:12:43,818 --> 00:12:45,439
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already rooted in craving.

135
00:12:55,980 --> 00:12:59,245
[SPEAKER_02]: So, don't lose the context, for example, on the unpleasantness of restlessness.

136
00:13:00,588 --> 00:13:01,649
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it will become the energy.

137
00:13:03,092 --> 00:13:04,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the unpleasantness of kind of being dull.

138
00:13:05,516 --> 00:13:06,477
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

139
00:13:06,658 --> 00:13:08,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it will become tranquility.

140
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:08,681
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to...

141
00:13:09,157 --> 00:13:11,898
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have anything happening all the time, or all the time figuring things out.

142
00:13:12,278 --> 00:13:12,718
[SPEAKER_02]: Why is that?

143
00:13:13,218 --> 00:13:17,640
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why do I, by default, habitually, try to get rid of this unpleasantness?

144
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:23,642
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because you still prioritize your views, that I must be doing this, I must be doing that, I must be getting rid of this.

145
00:13:24,082 --> 00:13:29,644
[SPEAKER_02]: But even that is rooted in simply wanting to be free from suffering by being free from craving.

146
00:13:29,664 --> 00:13:33,505
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why do you go around creating craving that makes you suffer, in order to be free from it?

147
00:13:35,733 --> 00:13:47,506
[SPEAKER_02]: Initially, yeah, everybody has to do that, but the point is to come when you recognize that, okay, now, if the mind is free from craving, all I need to do is stay mindful with that, and then make sure that it doesn't turn into craving.

148
00:13:47,786 --> 00:13:48,447
[SPEAKER_02]: You protect it.

149
00:13:48,967 --> 00:13:53,753
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to constantly sort of prod it, and prod it, and prod it, because again, it will become too tight.

150
00:13:57,608 --> 00:14:01,110
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, what do you think in terms of, like, things that people can pick up on?

151
00:14:01,130 --> 00:14:05,553
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it says, like, picking up the signs of when the mind is too much like this or too much like that.

152
00:14:06,413 --> 00:14:10,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, obviously, you know, inevitably, people are going to make the mistakes and go too far one way or the other.

153
00:14:11,036 --> 00:14:17,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, it seems that the Buddha's saying is important to, like, recognize, oh, when this is in fact happening, discern when that is happening.

154
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:18,000
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

155
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:23,544
[SPEAKER_02]: When you discern when that's happening, when you discern the context of your action, of what you're about to do, of what you're engaged in,

156
00:14:25,252 --> 00:14:26,914
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't, like, preemptively discern it.

157
00:14:27,014 --> 00:14:28,015
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, look, look, look, it's here.

158
00:14:28,996 --> 00:14:30,617
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's not necessarily a problem in itself.

159
00:14:31,298 --> 00:14:39,045
[SPEAKER_02]: The problem is, as I said, when, on account of your energy slash restlessness, you try to get rid of the pain through acting out of pain.

160
00:14:40,146 --> 00:14:43,630
[SPEAKER_02]: So you maintain the craving in regard to it, which is going to make you more restless.

161
00:14:44,350 --> 00:14:49,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're... it requires it for its own... for that very action.

162
00:14:51,746 --> 00:14:53,868
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you discern what's present.

163
00:14:54,088 --> 00:14:55,029
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is this my situation?

164
00:14:55,089 --> 00:14:58,692
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I trying to get rid of... Am I trying to understand craving now?

165
00:14:58,712 --> 00:15:01,914
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I just want to get rid of the discomfort of restlessness.

166
00:15:01,994 --> 00:15:07,179
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, am I trying to understand the dhamma because it's really unpleasant when my mind is like not thinking about anything?

167
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:08,880
[SPEAKER_02]: No engagement.

168
00:15:09,541 --> 00:15:10,281
[SPEAKER_02]: It frightens me.

169
00:15:11,582 --> 00:15:14,625
[SPEAKER_02]: Discerning that, it's already a step forward to not act out of it.

170
00:15:16,423 --> 00:15:16,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

171
00:15:16,643 --> 00:15:19,885
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't, like, make a checklist of signs to pick up...

172
00:15:19,945 --> 00:15:20,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

173
00:15:20,445 --> 00:15:20,765
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not.

174
00:15:20,805 --> 00:15:21,105
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

175
00:15:21,145 --> 00:15:22,026
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what signs are.

176
00:15:22,186 --> 00:15:25,387
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they're, like, these generic names, signs and tokens.

177
00:15:25,507 --> 00:15:27,308
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just characteristics of what's there.

178
00:15:27,328 --> 00:15:28,529
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is the main context?

179
00:15:29,109 --> 00:15:33,631
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the characteristic of the main context here is, fundamentally, I'm just acting out of suffering.

180
00:15:35,292 --> 00:15:38,093
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm trying to get rid of this unpleasant state of

181
00:15:39,855 --> 00:15:40,215
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever.

182
00:15:40,456 --> 00:15:44,660
[SPEAKER_02]: Dullness, non-thinking and whatnot, just mind is a bit slow.

183
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:45,540
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't like it.

184
00:15:45,661 --> 00:15:47,662
[SPEAKER_02]: I feel guilty, feel bad.

185
00:15:47,702 --> 00:15:49,144
[SPEAKER_02]: I feel I must do this, I must do that.

186
00:15:49,584 --> 00:15:49,824
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

187
00:15:49,844 --> 00:15:50,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Because this is unpleasant.

188
00:15:52,447 --> 00:15:58,833
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you end up acting out of the same principle that people act throughout their life, which is a principle that keeps you bound to the action.

189
00:16:00,455 --> 00:16:02,117
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's when you recognize... No, hold on.

190
00:16:02,558 --> 00:16:03,159
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not acting...

191
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:06,584
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not engaging in anything by wholesome, by body, by speech, by mind.

192
00:16:07,285 --> 00:16:09,748
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the dullness of the mind is not my dullness.

193
00:16:10,810 --> 00:16:12,052
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't need to be my problem.

194
00:16:12,232 --> 00:16:14,775
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a phenomenon there, as the current state of mind.

195
00:16:15,662 --> 00:16:17,643
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when satipaṭṭhāna sutta is done rightly.

196
00:16:18,083 --> 00:16:20,324
[SPEAKER_02]: The mindfulness on the level of the mind would be...

197
00:16:20,644 --> 00:16:23,266
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows his mind as elated mind.

198
00:16:23,286 --> 00:16:24,486
[SPEAKER_02]: Elated mind as elated mind.

199
00:16:24,546 --> 00:16:27,488
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows his depressed mind as depressed mind.

200
00:16:28,048 --> 00:16:29,769
[SPEAKER_02]: He knows his dull mind as dull mind.

201
00:16:30,189 --> 00:16:31,270
[SPEAKER_02]: Quick mind as quick mind.

202
00:16:31,350 --> 00:16:32,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Sharp, sharp, slow, slow.

203
00:16:32,450 --> 00:16:34,051
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter.

204
00:16:34,071 --> 00:16:35,112
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a state of mind.

205
00:16:35,772 --> 00:16:41,114
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's you who tries to get rid of it on account of discomfort it might cause you.

206
00:16:41,134 --> 00:16:44,096
[SPEAKER_02]: So, stop acting from out of the way you feel.

207
00:16:46,853 --> 00:16:54,539
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same principle that's kind of common to energy turning into restlessness or calmness turning into sloth and torpor.

208
00:16:56,581 --> 00:16:59,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Losing the context in regard to how it makes you feel.

209
00:17:01,622 --> 00:17:09,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Right when you said that, I realized that I had been just fighting off dullness the whole time of this talk, and that's why I was having trouble formulating more questions.

210
00:17:09,267 --> 00:17:10,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was like, why is this happening?

211
00:17:11,728 --> 00:17:12,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

212
00:17:12,729 --> 00:17:14,410
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes you're slower, sometimes you're faster.

213
00:17:14,430 --> 00:17:14,970
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter.

214
00:17:15,651 --> 00:17:17,752
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is losing the context on account of it.

215
00:17:18,552 --> 00:17:21,334
[SPEAKER_02]: And you lose the context when you choose to engage.

216
00:17:21,394 --> 00:17:22,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I must get rid of this dullness.

217
00:17:22,675 --> 00:17:24,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, I'll give in to the dullness.

218
00:17:25,497 --> 00:17:28,278
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, I must get rid of the... the...

219
00:17:29,664 --> 00:17:31,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Restlessness or I'll give in to restlessness.

220
00:17:31,645 --> 00:17:32,666
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, you engage with it.

221
00:17:33,947 --> 00:17:38,870
[SPEAKER_02]: How about I can stay mindful in regard to how I feel about the restlessness or while I'm restless.

222
00:17:39,371 --> 00:17:42,152
[SPEAKER_02]: How I feel while I'm slow.

223
00:17:43,353 --> 00:17:45,114
[SPEAKER_02]: How I feel while I'm doubtful.

224
00:17:46,475 --> 00:17:48,497
[SPEAKER_02]: How I feel while there is central inclination.

225
00:17:49,817 --> 00:17:56,202
[SPEAKER_02]: Because either... that's what's in common to all the hindrances, by the way, is acting on account of pain.

226
00:17:57,002 --> 00:17:58,083
[SPEAKER_02]: Sensuality is unpleasant.

227
00:17:58,670 --> 00:18:00,291
[SPEAKER_02]: You act towards it, to get rid of it.

228
00:18:01,252 --> 00:18:08,839
[SPEAKER_02]: It will... it's like a sting, it's unpleasant, so you want to act out, lash out, to get rid of it, to replace it, to retaliate.

229
00:18:09,079 --> 00:18:13,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, if... if it were not felt unpleasantly, you wouldn't be acting out of it.

230
00:18:16,426 --> 00:18:18,047
[SPEAKER_02]: And same for the other hindrances, doubt as well.

231
00:18:18,127 --> 00:18:25,874
[SPEAKER_02]: You try to figure things out, not because they need figuring out or because you lack clarity or not enough information about it.

232
00:18:25,914 --> 00:18:28,576
[SPEAKER_02]: It's simply because doubtful state is very unpleasant.

233
00:18:29,016 --> 00:18:40,326
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're just trying to get rid of how you feel on one level by acting on a completely different level of figuring this out that will then make me feel good.

234
00:18:41,506 --> 00:18:45,590
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if it does so, you still acted out of the way you felt.

235
00:18:46,666 --> 00:18:54,168
[SPEAKER_02]: And as such, you still remain fully pressured, subjected, contacted by the feeling, which means you always will be, for as long as you keep maintaining that type of action.

236
00:18:55,548 --> 00:18:59,229
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to learn how to act without needing to act out of your feeling.

237
00:19:01,250 --> 00:19:06,592
[SPEAKER_02]: And you learn how to act without needing to act out of your feeling by recognizing what's already there that doesn't require you or your action.

238
00:19:07,572 --> 00:19:08,732
[SPEAKER_02]: Dull mind is already there.

239
00:19:09,192 --> 00:19:11,233
[SPEAKER_02]: Sharp mind is already there at times.

240
00:19:11,313 --> 00:19:12,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Pleasant feeling is already there.

241
00:19:12,673 --> 00:19:13,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Unpleasant feeling is already there.

242
00:19:13,914 --> 00:19:15,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Look, body is already there.

243
00:19:16,343 --> 00:19:19,206
[SPEAKER_02]: You're holding the tablet, scratching your arm, whatever.

244
00:19:19,226 --> 00:19:19,926
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not thinking.

245
00:19:19,946 --> 00:19:21,247
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to attend to every second.

246
00:19:21,307 --> 00:19:22,108
[SPEAKER_02]: It's already happening.

247
00:19:23,109 --> 00:19:25,051
[SPEAKER_02]: And you don't need to stop it in order to recognize it.

248
00:19:25,311 --> 00:19:37,322
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to realize that there's a whole domain of things already happening that do not require your volition, your interference, your greed, your aversion, your delusion, your views, your assumptions.

249
00:19:38,002 --> 00:19:38,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Completely redundant.

250
00:19:40,592 --> 00:19:48,816
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's learning to see that domain as actually more fundamental than the domain of your volition, your choices, your wishes, your desires, your assumptions, and so on.

251
00:19:51,217 --> 00:19:54,078
[SPEAKER_01]: You said to check how you feel in regards to a thing.

252
00:19:54,098 --> 00:19:55,158
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not so much saying like...

253
00:19:55,719 --> 00:20:00,881
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, it's almost obvious that, yeah, it's going to be painful, but like, how do I feel about the pain is more important perhaps.

254
00:20:01,161 --> 00:20:01,941
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

255
00:20:01,961 --> 00:20:02,982
[SPEAKER_02]: Like what I asked you the other day.

256
00:20:03,422 --> 00:20:04,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, so what's your kind of mood?

257
00:20:04,363 --> 00:20:05,863
[SPEAKER_02]: My kind of mood is a bit unpleasant.

258
00:20:06,303 --> 00:20:07,504
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you feel about that mood?

259
00:20:08,537 --> 00:20:11,478
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you feel like, oh, this mood is unpleasant and I must understand it?

260
00:20:11,998 --> 00:20:13,998
[SPEAKER_02]: Means that's what your mood is, really.

261
00:20:14,238 --> 00:20:16,878
[SPEAKER_02]: It's how you feel in regard to your mood, is where your mood is.

262
00:20:18,339 --> 00:20:20,859
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought you were just saying, maybe you aren't so bothered by the pain.

263
00:20:20,899 --> 00:20:23,100
[SPEAKER_01]: At that time, you might say, oh, I feel kind of okay about it.

264
00:20:23,140 --> 00:20:23,520
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fine.

265
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:24,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's okay.

266
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,901
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's unpleasant, but... doesn't need to be anything more than that.

267
00:20:30,061 --> 00:20:35,762
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless you lose the sight of it, and then... automatically start leaning towards trying to get rid of it.

268
00:20:48,396 --> 00:20:54,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, as far as these energies go, you know, we're talking about energy and energy and stuff, it's obviously...

269
00:20:54,298 --> 00:20:55,438
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing mystical about it.

270
00:20:55,578 --> 00:20:56,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

271
00:20:57,258 --> 00:21:02,560
[SPEAKER_01]: But in terms of, like, the, you know, when the Buddha talked about energy, then what are we exactly referring to?

272
00:21:02,620 --> 00:21:05,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll read the four right strivings there.

273
00:21:05,841 --> 00:21:06,581
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

274
00:21:06,661 --> 00:21:08,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Effort, willingness, readiness for effort.

275
00:21:09,962 --> 00:21:10,802
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what energy is.

276
00:21:11,397 --> 00:21:13,817
[SPEAKER_01]: It's almost like not always the act of preparedness.

277
00:21:14,117 --> 00:21:15,078
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, you're ready.

278
00:21:15,558 --> 00:21:16,338
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I can...

279
00:21:16,998 --> 00:21:20,199
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to spend three hours thinking, oh, I need to do this work.

280
00:21:20,239 --> 00:21:23,379
[SPEAKER_02]: No, just, oh, work needs to be done, I do it.

281
00:21:23,419 --> 00:21:24,419
[SPEAKER_02]: No work needs to be done.

282
00:21:24,439 --> 00:21:25,420
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm still energetic.

283
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:30,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Without needing to act and kind of engage with things because I'm so full of energy.

284
00:21:30,941 --> 00:21:31,101
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

285
00:21:31,701 --> 00:21:34,281
[SPEAKER_02]: You have energy means you are ready to act properly.

286
00:21:35,321 --> 00:21:37,022
[SPEAKER_02]: If lust, you're ready to not entertain it.

287
00:21:37,102 --> 00:21:38,622
[SPEAKER_02]: If non-lust, you're ready to protect it.

288
00:21:39,902 --> 00:21:42,905
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why energy, rightly, with the right context, it's effortless.

289
00:21:43,526 --> 00:21:44,307
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all you're doing.

290
00:21:44,327 --> 00:21:46,289
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

291
00:21:46,329 --> 00:21:47,731
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how is this tireless energy?

292
00:21:48,592 --> 00:21:49,933
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what tireless energy is exactly.

293
00:21:49,953 --> 00:21:50,614
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't tire you.

294
00:21:51,435 --> 00:21:53,977
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to be tiring yourself out of that energy.

295
00:21:54,598 --> 00:21:57,982
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just endless energy because it's... it's an endless readiness.

296
00:22:06,513 --> 00:22:08,614
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is a state of mind independent of your action.

297
00:22:08,834 --> 00:22:11,956
[SPEAKER_02]: It depends on your understanding and the context, the clarity of the context.

298
00:22:25,982 --> 00:22:28,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's four great strivings that...

299
00:22:29,494 --> 00:22:34,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Here bhikkhus, a bhikkhu generates desire for the non-arising of unarisen evil and wholesome states.

300
00:22:35,417 --> 00:22:39,278
[SPEAKER_01]: He makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind and strives.

301
00:22:39,959 --> 00:22:42,980
[SPEAKER_01]: He generates for the abandoning of arisen evil and wholesome states.

302
00:22:43,580 --> 00:22:45,101
[SPEAKER_01]: He makes an effort, so on.

303
00:22:45,922 --> 00:22:48,703
[SPEAKER_01]: He generates for the arising of unarisen wholesome states.

304
00:22:49,183 --> 00:22:51,304
[SPEAKER_01]: He makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind.

305
00:22:52,055 --> 00:22:59,782
[SPEAKER_01]: He generates desire for the maintenance of arisen wholesome states, for their non-decay, increase, expansion, and fulfillment by development.

306
00:23:00,342 --> 00:23:04,686
[SPEAKER_01]: These are the four right strivings.

307
00:23:04,726 --> 00:23:11,091
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you mentioned, like, an effortless energy preparedness kind of thing.

308
00:23:11,111 --> 00:23:15,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's to say...

309
00:23:25,697 --> 00:23:28,698
[SPEAKER_01]: I lost the question, because mine is still there.

310
00:23:28,938 --> 00:23:31,800
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, no, it's all right.

311
00:23:32,940 --> 00:23:48,087
[SPEAKER_02]: If done rightly, arousing that energy against the backdrop of the right context, it's same like with the arising of maintenance of mindfulness.

312
00:23:48,367 --> 00:23:50,328
[SPEAKER_02]: It's effortless and it's also not unpleasant.

313
00:23:51,749 --> 00:24:04,352
[SPEAKER_02]: If you need to arouse the energy and it feels like a chore and it's unpleasant, means there is the element of restlessness that bothers you, means you're acting out of craving.

314
00:24:06,952 --> 00:24:08,153
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, why?

315
00:24:08,213 --> 00:24:10,213
[SPEAKER_02]: You ask yourself, why do you need to arouse energy then?

316
00:24:10,613 --> 00:24:14,194
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, regain the context for energy.

317
00:24:14,214 --> 00:24:19,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Not sense of duty or because somebody said or because I must be energetic.

318
00:24:20,438 --> 00:24:21,899
[SPEAKER_02]: Then it's... you can't sustain that.

319
00:24:22,540 --> 00:24:23,200
[SPEAKER_02]: Then it's like, why?

320
00:24:24,201 --> 00:24:25,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do I need to arise energy?

321
00:24:25,562 --> 00:24:26,242
[SPEAKER_02]: And when it says why?

322
00:24:27,783 --> 00:24:29,745
[SPEAKER_02]: To dispel the unwholesome states.

323
00:24:29,785 --> 00:24:31,886
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, so are there unwholesome states that need dispelling?

324
00:24:32,327 --> 00:24:32,547
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

325
00:24:33,147 --> 00:24:34,528
[SPEAKER_02]: So what am I arising energy for?

326
00:24:35,509 --> 00:24:36,630
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe I'm already energetic.

327
00:24:36,670 --> 00:24:39,111
[SPEAKER_02]: I just don't see it because I turn it into restlessness by default.

328
00:24:40,452 --> 00:24:42,594
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, I am ready to deal with things when they come.

329
00:24:43,234 --> 00:24:45,176
[SPEAKER_02]: That doesn't mean you keep going inviting them to come.

330
00:24:46,637 --> 00:24:47,237
[SPEAKER_02]: Quite the opposite.

331
00:24:47,858 --> 00:24:51,740
[SPEAKER_02]: You learn to recognize, when they're not there, then, okay, now it's a different energy.

332
00:24:51,780 --> 00:24:53,981
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not the energy, I must deal with this, because there's nothing to deal with it.

333
00:24:54,041 --> 00:24:55,001
[SPEAKER_02]: I just must protect this.

334
00:24:55,802 --> 00:24:58,983
[SPEAKER_02]: And you protect it, by not trying to deal with it.

335
00:25:00,964 --> 00:25:02,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're getting a sense of, like, sometimes...

336
00:25:03,006 --> 00:25:07,268
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it almost seems like an obvious thing, like, you know, you're a little bit dull, a little bit tired.

337
00:25:07,308 --> 00:25:12,490
[SPEAKER_01]: The obvious thing that surfaces, you just kind of rouse yourself, but it's not always necessarily the right thing to be doing.

338
00:25:12,830 --> 00:25:13,411
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

339
00:25:13,491 --> 00:25:14,811
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like passing out or something.

340
00:25:14,931 --> 00:25:15,272
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

341
00:25:15,312 --> 00:25:17,332
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I feel drowsy and tired.

342
00:25:17,913 --> 00:25:18,233
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

343
00:25:22,375 --> 00:25:23,175
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not sure why.

344
00:25:23,955 --> 00:25:24,756
[SPEAKER_02]: I just feel that way.

345
00:25:24,856 --> 00:25:25,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

346
00:25:25,196 --> 00:25:27,517
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is it a problem?

347
00:25:29,338 --> 00:25:31,059
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I giving in to lust on account of this?

348
00:25:31,459 --> 00:25:31,639
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

349
00:25:32,672 --> 00:25:34,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I giving in to anger, ill will on account of this?

350
00:25:35,134 --> 00:25:35,294
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

351
00:25:35,935 --> 00:25:39,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I performing actions of distracting nature on account of it?

352
00:25:39,558 --> 00:25:39,739
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

353
00:25:39,979 --> 00:25:41,040
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, that's all I need to do.

354
00:25:41,680 --> 00:25:42,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Not give in to these things.

355
00:25:42,942 --> 00:25:45,204
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to get rid of this, because I didn't have seen its arising.

356
00:25:45,724 --> 00:25:48,567
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you realize, maybe because you didn't sleep well last night.

357
00:25:48,587 --> 00:25:49,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe because you're tired.

358
00:25:49,588 --> 00:25:50,569
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe because you didn't eat enough.

359
00:25:51,069 --> 00:25:51,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe because you're sick.

360
00:25:51,930 --> 00:25:53,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Who knows what the reason is?

361
00:25:53,391 --> 00:25:53,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Doesn't matter.

362
00:25:54,452 --> 00:25:56,114
[SPEAKER_02]: What matters is why do you act out of it.

363
00:25:57,316 --> 00:25:59,377
[SPEAKER_02]: I must always get rid of the fatigue and tiredness.

364
00:25:59,777 --> 00:26:02,578
[SPEAKER_02]: It's impossible, because the body gets fatigued and tired.

365
00:26:05,480 --> 00:26:10,942
[SPEAKER_02]: You just don't want to act out of, ah... losing the context.

366
00:26:11,662 --> 00:26:14,524
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't want to act out of habit, out of a duty, out of a whatever.

367
00:26:16,244 --> 00:26:24,128
[SPEAKER_02]: It's usually a habit, because in the beginning you don't really can't see the threshold of the hindrances, so then everything and nothing is a problem.

368
00:26:24,931 --> 00:26:26,012
[SPEAKER_02]: When the problem is there, you don't see it.

369
00:26:26,032 --> 00:26:28,613
[SPEAKER_02]: When the problem is not there, you make it into a problem.

370
00:26:30,694 --> 00:26:33,336
[SPEAKER_02]: So, inevitably, you have to work your way through that.

371
00:26:33,396 --> 00:26:38,879
[SPEAKER_02]: But the point has to come when you recognize that, yeah, okay, I'm dull, I'm tired, I can't think.

372
00:26:38,899 --> 00:26:39,580
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not the problem.

373
00:26:39,640 --> 00:26:48,505
[SPEAKER_02]: The problem is when I choose to act towards greed, towards ill-will, aversion, hatred, towards distraction, carelessness, out of this feeling.

374
00:26:49,625 --> 00:26:51,286
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's a clear choice.

375
00:26:51,306 --> 00:26:52,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you just don't act there.

376
00:26:52,687 --> 00:26:53,768
[SPEAKER_02]: And you don't need to do anything with it.

377
00:26:56,021 --> 00:26:59,383
[SPEAKER_02]: You mentioned before, like... And, by the way, same if, like, I must figure this out.

378
00:27:01,704 --> 00:27:01,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

379
00:27:02,084 --> 00:27:03,725
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, because I want to get rid of it.

380
00:27:04,185 --> 00:27:06,306
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm acting out of it, just on the level of the mind.

381
00:27:08,367 --> 00:27:15,370
[SPEAKER_01]: And you mentioned, like, if there's doubt or ambiguity, it's kind of probably on that side of things.

382
00:27:15,390 --> 00:27:15,770
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you mean?

383
00:27:16,450 --> 00:27:19,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you want to... you want to accomplish something, you want to do something.

384
00:27:19,812 --> 00:27:20,052
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, like,

385
00:27:20,965 --> 00:27:25,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Almost like you're finding different reasons, so you're not really finding the right reason.

386
00:27:25,287 --> 00:27:26,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, sure, sure.

387
00:27:26,328 --> 00:27:27,568
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not even clear as to why.

388
00:27:27,608 --> 00:27:29,189
[SPEAKER_02]: You just keep looking for something to do.

389
00:27:29,229 --> 00:27:32,631
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you just look for... almost like you're just in your mind starting to give justifications.

390
00:27:32,651 --> 00:27:34,892
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, well, that's like acting out of doubt, yeah.

391
00:27:34,912 --> 00:27:43,597
[SPEAKER_02]: See, like, you can, as I said many times before, you can only doubt the content of your experience, of your thoughts, of the idea, whatever you're doubtful about.

392
00:27:43,997 --> 00:27:49,179
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can't doubt that you're experiencing nature of doubt, that you're under the pressure of doubt.

393
00:27:49,259 --> 00:27:49,600
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot

394
00:27:52,394 --> 00:28:07,126
[SPEAKER_02]: But losing the sight that that's what's first, that that's where the problem is, you then give in to try to clarify the content, deal with the content, re-explain the content, revisit the content, re-read the content, hash out the content again, because it doesn't make me feel right.

395
00:28:08,707 --> 00:28:10,589
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't make you feel right, because doubt is unpleasant.

396
00:28:11,149 --> 00:28:11,509
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

397
00:28:12,170 --> 00:28:13,231
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't want to feel unpleasant.

398
00:28:27,450 --> 00:28:32,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's really a question of... well, you should... if you're being affected by the doubt, that's the first question.

399
00:28:32,392 --> 00:28:35,453
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it is, and that's... that's the work that you've been mentioning.

400
00:28:35,973 --> 00:28:39,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the work sounds like it's not what you assume the work has to be at any given time.

401
00:28:39,714 --> 00:28:39,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

402
00:28:40,034 --> 00:28:40,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

403
00:28:40,554 --> 00:28:40,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

404
00:28:41,635 --> 00:28:45,636
[SPEAKER_01]: You may well be, like, looking past where the work already is.

405
00:28:45,656 --> 00:28:45,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

406
00:28:46,136 --> 00:28:46,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

407
00:28:46,616 --> 00:28:48,297
[SPEAKER_02]: More often than not, I think that's the case.

408
00:28:48,977 --> 00:28:51,238
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you act out of this... or this is what... No.

409
00:28:51,478 --> 00:28:53,999
[SPEAKER_02]: My work is determined by what's presently enduring.

410
00:28:54,838 --> 00:28:57,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by what my sense of duty dictates.

411
00:28:58,161 --> 00:28:59,662
[SPEAKER_02]: And even it's like, okay, so sense of duty.

412
00:28:59,702 --> 00:29:01,163
[SPEAKER_02]: How did I acquire that sense of duty?

413
00:29:01,463 --> 00:29:03,945
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize you acquired it before you were aware of any context.

414
00:29:03,985 --> 00:29:08,569
[SPEAKER_02]: When you just adopted it as as a must, on account of faith, on account of external instruction.

415
00:29:08,609 --> 00:29:13,993
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is fine, but if you remain bound to it, you remain bound to sīla bhārmasa.

416
00:29:15,234 --> 00:29:20,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Acting out of the sense of duty without actually discernment of what's presently there and what presently it's addressing.

417
00:29:22,003 --> 00:29:25,166
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means you won't be seeing the mind free from lust as mind free from lust.

418
00:29:25,266 --> 00:29:27,788
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't be seeing mind affected with lust as mind affected with lust.

419
00:29:28,028 --> 00:29:29,109
[SPEAKER_02]: And with anger and distraction.

420
00:29:29,970 --> 00:29:33,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're just acting out sense of duty, always, regardless of what the state of mind is.

421
00:29:35,174 --> 00:29:37,436
[SPEAKER_02]: And by doing so, you end up appropriating your state of mind.

422
00:30:15,190 --> 00:30:17,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have anything you wanted to bring?

423
00:30:20,114 --> 00:30:29,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, I've seen the translation for energy as zeal, and it made me think of the first sutta you had read.

424
00:30:29,962 --> 00:30:44,334
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, seeing a teacher or accomplished bhikkhus, instead of, like, having this arousal of energy, more of a zeal, in the sense of confidence,

425
00:30:45,512 --> 00:30:46,713
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, recognizing.

426
00:30:46,733 --> 00:30:59,864
[SPEAKER_02]: That's like in the other suttas, when it says, you know, a man interested in his own welfare, he goes and visits wise men, and then he lends the ear, understand what they say, then he measures the task.

427
00:30:59,904 --> 00:31:01,666
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like a prerequisite for the zeal.

428
00:31:02,526 --> 00:31:04,608
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this is what needs to be done.

429
00:31:04,628 --> 00:31:06,910
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't be zealous about a task when you have no idea.

430
00:31:07,150 --> 00:31:08,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'll just do everything and nothing.

431
00:31:09,472 --> 00:31:10,053
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll just pray.

432
00:31:11,461 --> 00:31:13,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Meditate, watch my nostril, and then the work will be done for me.

433
00:31:13,964 --> 00:31:17,867
[SPEAKER_02]: You know exactly, you know exactly what needs to be done to free yourself from suffering.

434
00:31:18,368 --> 00:31:19,529
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, so, that's it.

435
00:31:20,030 --> 00:31:22,872
[SPEAKER_02]: It's doable by knowing what needs to be done, you make it doable.

436
00:31:24,677 --> 00:31:26,378
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the energy will arise from it, yeah.

437
00:31:27,258 --> 00:31:30,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Could that also be like a sense of urgency?

438
00:31:30,579 --> 00:31:32,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Like seeing that... Well, say, yeah.

439
00:31:32,220 --> 00:31:37,041
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I think sense of urgency would be more of like a, you know, a prod that would result in energy.

440
00:31:38,102 --> 00:31:39,762
[SPEAKER_02]: But yeah, yeah, it's connected to that.

441
00:31:40,163 --> 00:31:41,343
[SPEAKER_02]: It's exactly connected to that.

442
00:31:41,483 --> 00:31:45,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, like, oh, no, just, oh, there's this world that needs to be done, but I can afford to not do it.

443
00:31:46,505 --> 00:31:52,227
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you recognize, oh, it needs to be done, and now I know what it is, and it's like, it's a must.

444
00:31:53,061 --> 00:31:53,821
[SPEAKER_02]: It's an imperative.

445
00:31:54,001 --> 00:31:54,722
[SPEAKER_02]: You will have to do it.

446
00:31:56,202 --> 00:32:03,505
[SPEAKER_02]: But because now you recognize for what it is, it doesn't feel like this ambiguous sort of non-defined chore that oppresses you, but you can't really put your finger on it.

447
00:32:04,485 --> 00:32:07,186
[SPEAKER_01]: You know exactly what suffering is, what freedom from suffering is.

448
00:32:07,286 --> 00:32:07,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Psst!

449
00:32:08,146 --> 00:32:08,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't eat the leech.

450
00:32:09,887 --> 00:32:14,449
[SPEAKER_02]: You know exactly what suffering is, what freedom from suffering is, and you know exactly what to do to develop it.

451
00:32:18,550 --> 00:32:20,951
[SPEAKER_02]: So that, yeah, that's why the right view needs to come first.

452
00:32:22,005 --> 00:32:24,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, there is no right effort without right view.

453
00:32:30,708 --> 00:32:43,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Then people... then the effort, if it's not in line with right view, the effort is wrong, and it could be just because of that, sort of like, oh, I feel unpleasant feelings.

454
00:32:43,811 --> 00:32:48,973
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you know... if you have that understanding of that,

455
00:32:50,182 --> 00:32:55,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're sitting close to that, yeah.

456
00:33:10,238 --> 00:33:13,059
[SPEAKER_02]: But not just, oh, I must, I must act out of this, get rid of this.

457
00:33:13,079 --> 00:33:18,020
[SPEAKER_02]: You recognize that you're acting on account of how you feel, which means craving has to be there.

458
00:33:18,040 --> 00:33:20,781
[SPEAKER_02]: You, you know, you can't directly seek, oh, this is my craving.

459
00:33:21,442 --> 00:33:24,783
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can know based on the inclination towards the present enduring feeling.

460
00:33:25,203 --> 00:33:26,303
[SPEAKER_02]: Am I trying to get rid of this?

461
00:33:26,343 --> 00:33:27,583
[SPEAKER_02]: Means I am craving against it.

462
00:33:28,164 --> 00:33:29,944
[SPEAKER_02]: I am the craving against it.

463
00:33:30,985 --> 00:33:32,285
[SPEAKER_02]: I am the craving for it.

464
00:33:33,947 --> 00:33:40,230
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why you can't just... oh, just be present, just be here, just always do the same thing, because it's not... mind is not always in the same state.

465
00:33:41,931 --> 00:33:49,315
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can act for the right reason, but not... but acting just because of the feeling, that is the wrong reason.

466
00:33:49,756 --> 00:33:50,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

467
00:33:50,156 --> 00:33:50,996
[SPEAKER_02]: That's going to make it wrong.

468
00:33:51,016 --> 00:33:55,539
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you're still not going to be freeing yourself from craving.

469
00:33:55,559 --> 00:33:59,081
[SPEAKER_02]: You might, oh, well, it's my duty to contemplate and do walking meditation.

470
00:33:59,141 --> 00:33:59,581
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, why?

471
00:34:00,758 --> 00:34:10,585
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you need something to do, because you're restless, because it's unpleasant, because you want to get rid of the pain, or because you really want to maintain the state that's free from craving, that you understood so.

472
00:34:16,130 --> 00:34:21,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That seems like a lot of the work to me, like making... working to not have work, so to speak.

473
00:34:21,954 --> 00:34:24,556
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's why you start doing it rightly, it ceases to be work.

474
00:34:24,696 --> 00:34:29,620
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you say, oh, they practice the dhamma, you know, full of joy and delight, because it's effortless.

475
00:34:30,948 --> 00:34:43,693
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, again, you invest the work in adjusting the strings of your lute, but once you get them right, it's just effortless.

476
00:34:43,753 --> 00:34:44,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Music just flows.

477
00:34:46,574 --> 00:34:48,695
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you only adjust it when it needs to be adjusted.

478
00:34:49,935 --> 00:34:54,577
[SPEAKER_02]: Not all, just part of you playing is adjusting it, and that's not playing.

479
00:34:58,423 --> 00:35:11,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, for example, let's say, the sutta, like, there's this one, the Majjhima-Nikāya, I don't have it with me here, the one where I mentioned, like, the, you know, the ways of stilling or calming, I don't know how you want to... Ah, removal of distracting thoughts.

480
00:35:12,172 --> 00:35:13,192
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how it's translated.

481
00:35:13,212 --> 00:35:13,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

482
00:35:13,813 --> 00:35:19,236
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, that one, I think, a lot of people kind of misinterpret that as, like...

483
00:35:19,256 --> 00:35:20,517
[SPEAKER_01]: I do this when this is here.

484
00:35:20,557 --> 00:35:21,738
[SPEAKER_02]: I do that... Yeah, exactly.

485
00:35:21,758 --> 00:35:24,599
[SPEAKER_02]: That means, either way, you keep acting on account of unpleasantness or

486
00:35:26,629 --> 00:35:28,990
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is on the level of doing, which is why it's unpleasant.

487
00:35:29,270 --> 00:35:29,731
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a duty.

488
00:35:29,751 --> 00:35:30,311
[SPEAKER_02]: I have to do it.

489
00:35:30,731 --> 00:35:31,972
[SPEAKER_02]: I have to respond to this.

490
00:35:33,353 --> 00:35:34,714
[SPEAKER_02]: And that way, it's always going to be a burden.

491
00:35:35,794 --> 00:35:37,936
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you do it rightly, it's not on the level of doing.

492
00:35:39,457 --> 00:35:41,478
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of discerning, which is effortless.

493
00:35:41,558 --> 00:35:43,620
[SPEAKER_02]: Because things you discern are already there.

494
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:49,984
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to develop them, create them, or, like, you know, bring them back or something.

495
00:35:50,004 --> 00:35:50,444
[SPEAKER_02]: They're there.

496
00:35:50,544 --> 00:35:54,987
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to stop distracting yourself from them, through your efforts of doing them.

497
00:35:56,912 --> 00:35:58,733
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why people try too hard to be mindful.

498
00:35:58,773 --> 00:35:59,633
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you're not mindful.

499
00:36:00,493 --> 00:36:01,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're looking at the wrong place.

500
00:36:02,774 --> 00:36:04,375
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindfulness is not on the level of the doing.

501
00:36:04,395 --> 00:36:06,915
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of intention behind your doing.

502
00:36:07,696 --> 00:36:11,157
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of presently enduring feeling, which is a basis for your doing.

503
00:36:12,357 --> 00:36:16,098
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of the presently enduring mood or the body, which is a basis for your action.

504
00:36:16,659 --> 00:36:17,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, here you are.

505
00:36:17,359 --> 00:36:18,319
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm mindful.

506
00:36:18,579 --> 00:36:19,320
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm mindful.

507
00:36:19,480 --> 00:36:20,080
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm mindful.

508
00:36:20,100 --> 00:36:20,880
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm mindful.

509
00:36:21,180 --> 00:36:23,341
[SPEAKER_02]: You're doing it and that's why you're not mindful.

510
00:36:29,771 --> 00:36:52,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms that it sounds like it's something like, okay, you have... you are properly enduring whatever unwholesome thought has arisen, and then, kind of, these features, you've read about them, they will become apparent, because you're not... Yeah, because now you're providing the basis for those features to be discerned, because they are on those levels, but you were not seeing it, because you were not familiar enough.

511
00:36:55,225 --> 00:36:56,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in a sense of speaking, it's not like...

512
00:36:59,518 --> 00:37:05,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Choosing is not the right term, in terms of how people do you think I'm gonna choose now to respond like this.

513
00:37:06,864 --> 00:37:07,865
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, it's not.

514
00:37:08,366 --> 00:37:09,327
[SPEAKER_02]: It precedes your choice.

515
00:37:09,887 --> 00:37:12,049
[SPEAKER_02]: It even precedes your attention, in a way.

516
00:37:12,389 --> 00:37:21,017
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I keep saying, like, mindfulness is about discerning the context, knowing what's already there,

517
00:37:22,323 --> 00:37:25,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Without needing to attend to it, to know that's already there.

518
00:37:26,146 --> 00:37:30,710
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason you can attend to what you feel, is because you're already feeling it.

519
00:37:31,871 --> 00:37:34,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means there's already a recognition of what's being felt.

520
00:37:34,933 --> 00:37:40,117
[SPEAKER_02]: But you choose to ignore that, put that second, you choose to compromise that by allowing... No.

521
00:37:40,277 --> 00:37:43,240
[SPEAKER_02]: Still, when I attend to it, now I know it's there.

522
00:37:43,620 --> 00:37:44,881
[SPEAKER_02]: But you already knew what was there.

523
00:37:46,167 --> 00:37:46,847
[SPEAKER_02]: Same with the body.

524
00:37:47,368 --> 00:37:49,869
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to keep attending to yourself that you're seated now.

525
00:37:50,089 --> 00:37:50,870
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just seated.

526
00:37:51,290 --> 00:37:54,872
[SPEAKER_02]: And if I were to ask you, out of the blue, what's your body position?

527
00:37:55,533 --> 00:37:57,354
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm seated.

528
00:37:57,414 --> 00:38:01,536
[SPEAKER_02]: Now you can choose to attend to it as well, but the only reason is because you already know that you're seated.

529
00:38:02,597 --> 00:38:05,379
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's discerning that.

530
00:38:05,399 --> 00:38:10,141
[SPEAKER_02]: Knowledge without attending it.

531
00:38:10,161 --> 00:38:12,423
[SPEAKER_02]: So you have to resist the need to attend it.

532
00:38:13,432 --> 00:38:15,573
[SPEAKER_02]: And learn to discern what you already know and not...

533
00:38:16,013 --> 00:38:17,553
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I know, but I'll still attend it.

534
00:38:17,653 --> 00:38:17,833
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

535
00:38:18,413 --> 00:38:23,554
[SPEAKER_02]: That means you won't know it on the same level, because you chose still to act out of what you attend, as in what you chose.

536
00:38:24,394 --> 00:38:26,635
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you still want to make things to be on your own terms.

537
00:38:29,396 --> 00:38:33,296
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's that kind of compulsive bit that some people might need to restrain.

538
00:38:33,957 --> 00:38:40,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, even when you know, you still choose to give in to, okay, now let me attend it and reconfirm what I already know.

539
00:38:40,960 --> 00:38:47,603
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, oh, let me still allow myself to act out of how I feel, that I already know how I feel, but I want to act out of it anyway.

540
00:38:49,244 --> 00:38:55,386
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's your own dependence on your own craving that you're fully responsible for and you're maintaining through that type of action.

541
00:38:57,727 --> 00:39:00,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, that's like your recent talk about the mindful...

542
00:39:00,088 --> 00:39:02,069
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know how recent it is... mindful forgetting.

543
00:39:02,309 --> 00:39:04,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, forgetting properly means not remembering wrongly.

544
00:39:05,491 --> 00:39:06,191
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, exactly.

545
00:39:06,211 --> 00:39:07,152
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

546
00:39:07,272 --> 00:39:07,932
[SPEAKER_02]: It's related.

547
00:39:09,763 --> 00:39:20,866
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in terms of like, you know, mindfulness brings composure, that composure and that base, it's like on the level of that mindfulness, not that's something like you think, oh, now composure is like more known.

548
00:39:21,086 --> 00:39:21,706
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

549
00:39:21,906 --> 00:39:23,287
[SPEAKER_02]: Or more attentive or something.

550
00:39:23,327 --> 00:39:23,487
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

551
00:39:23,627 --> 00:39:26,567
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just more of an unshakable context.

552
00:39:27,408 --> 00:39:28,468
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what composure is.

553
00:39:29,290 --> 00:39:32,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, say, it's more, almost like, durable, it includes more.

554
00:39:32,912 --> 00:39:34,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

555
00:39:34,093 --> 00:39:34,953
[SPEAKER_02]: Less perturbable.

556
00:39:35,594 --> 00:39:37,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that it's always on your mind.

557
00:39:37,475 --> 00:39:38,015
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

558
00:39:38,075 --> 00:39:43,198
[SPEAKER_02]: It just remains there and it doesn't get perturbed by things you do attend or choose to do within that.

559
00:39:44,278 --> 00:39:52,063
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why an arahant, who has mindfulness that's imperturbable to its fullness, still speaks, talks, goes to toilet, eats, whatever.

560
00:39:52,163 --> 00:39:53,503
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot be discerned externally.

561
00:39:53,563 --> 00:39:55,865
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, look, he's gliding in the air because he's an arahant.

562
00:39:55,925 --> 00:39:57,005
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you can't tell him apart.

563
00:39:57,205 --> 00:39:57,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Often they couldn't.

564
00:39:58,645 --> 00:40:00,527
[SPEAKER_02]: Often other arahants couldn't tell him apart.

565
00:40:01,327 --> 00:40:03,809
[SPEAKER_02]: They had to talk and see what the understanding is, how to understand it.

566
00:40:04,210 --> 00:40:05,891
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, yeah, his mind is most likely free.

567
00:40:06,151 --> 00:40:07,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Even the Buddha couldn't always tell.

568
00:40:07,612 --> 00:40:21,484
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, yeah, he's free maybe, but if there's some residue left, you know, a little bit somewhere, unspecified, who knows to what extent, he'll be reborn in those great heavens and then become a non-returner.

569
00:40:23,565 --> 00:40:25,347
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's again, it's about

570
00:40:27,239 --> 00:40:47,391
[SPEAKER_02]: Developing that kind of context, discernment, which is what your mindfulness is, to the extent necessary, for, like, removal of any basis for acting out of greed, out of aversion, hatred, carelessness, out of distraction, boredom, all the unwholesome things.

571
00:40:47,491 --> 00:40:48,572
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why they're unwholesome.

572
00:40:59,535 --> 00:41:02,436
[SPEAKER_01]: And what would you... what would you refer to when you say carelessness?

573
00:41:03,076 --> 00:41:04,757
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you referring to there?

574
00:41:05,637 --> 00:41:08,798
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I think that, like, carelessness would be...

575
00:41:08,818 --> 00:41:10,358
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you know you're a bit muddled.

576
00:41:11,699 --> 00:41:16,960
[SPEAKER_02]: You know... there might be some, like, laziness present.

577
00:41:18,020 --> 00:41:20,841
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you lose context of that.

578
00:41:21,762 --> 00:41:26,303
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't necessarily go straight into, oh, I'll just do something lustful or angry.

579
00:41:27,142 --> 00:41:28,643
[SPEAKER_02]: But you start drifting away.

580
00:41:28,663 --> 00:41:31,704
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'll just go and hang out for the sake of chit-chat.

581
00:41:32,765 --> 00:41:34,285
[SPEAKER_02]: Not sensual chit-chat, but still.

582
00:41:35,126 --> 00:41:38,807
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'll just go and, you know, find some random work, because I need something to do.

583
00:41:38,907 --> 00:41:41,188
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already acting out of carelessness and distraction.

584
00:41:42,089 --> 00:41:44,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Almost like not clarifying your intentions enough.

585
00:41:44,770 --> 00:41:45,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

586
00:41:45,470 --> 00:41:47,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, I don't know, it's fine.

587
00:41:47,051 --> 00:41:48,212
[SPEAKER_01]: It'll be fine, or something.

588
00:41:48,532 --> 00:41:49,092
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, yeah.

589
00:41:49,232 --> 00:41:50,092
[SPEAKER_02]: Choosing to ignore.

590
00:41:50,112 --> 00:41:52,093
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I'll be alright.

591
00:41:52,133 --> 00:41:52,794
[SPEAKER_02]: I can handle it.

592
00:41:53,094 --> 00:41:55,635
[SPEAKER_02]: I can, I can, I can sort of, eh...

593
00:41:57,459 --> 00:41:58,120
[SPEAKER_02]: Absorb this.

594
00:41:58,640 --> 00:41:59,220
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll be fine.

595
00:41:59,441 --> 00:42:03,824
[SPEAKER_02]: But, see, the problem is that you lose the context on account of it, and that's why you're not fine.

596
00:42:03,964 --> 00:42:10,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you, yeah, don't go further in your careless actions, nevertheless, you gave in to that extent.

597
00:42:12,631 --> 00:42:24,560
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why, you know, even somebody who has understood the dhamma can can distract himself from fulfilling that understanding by work, what was the other one?

598
00:42:24,600 --> 00:42:25,541
[SPEAKER_02]: Sleep and talk.

599
00:42:25,701 --> 00:42:25,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

600
00:42:31,784 --> 00:42:35,627
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a lot of work that doesn't need to be... like, it's not end of the world or something.

601
00:42:35,987 --> 00:42:38,429
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it must be done and then you just enjoy doing it.

602
00:42:39,730 --> 00:42:45,195
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, like, building things up, repairing things that don't really... they're not to the point of repair.

603
00:42:45,255 --> 00:42:50,079
[SPEAKER_02]: Or just finding things to do, finding activity, finding engagement, finding projects.

604
00:42:52,830 --> 00:43:00,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the hard thing for me, like, kind of choosing when and where to actually start investigating my intentions, because there's almost...

605
00:43:00,694 --> 00:43:05,957
[SPEAKER_01]: I have this inclination that I have to check every thought, and it's just not possible, not sustainable.

606
00:43:06,198 --> 00:43:07,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, yeah, yeah.

607
00:43:07,358 --> 00:43:12,341
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just going to wind you up quite unpleasantly, because you keep trying to control the uncontrollable.

608
00:43:13,061 --> 00:43:14,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, let me check this, let me check... Why?

609
00:43:14,682 --> 00:43:15,963
[SPEAKER_02]: Why am I checking this?

610
00:43:16,263 --> 00:43:19,185
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let me attend to this, let me attend to this, let me attend to this, let me...

611
00:43:20,373 --> 00:43:21,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you attending to it?

612
00:43:21,654 --> 00:43:23,795
[SPEAKER_02]: To discern the knowledge that precedes your attention?

613
00:43:26,097 --> 00:43:33,081
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why more often than not, it's actually people too keen to get rid of things even at the pretext of practicing the dhamma.

614
00:43:34,262 --> 00:43:37,284
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why calmness requires learning.

615
00:43:37,324 --> 00:43:42,968
[SPEAKER_02]: Like samatha requires learning, requires understanding what samatha is, as the suttas often say.

616
00:43:44,097 --> 00:43:48,320
[SPEAKER_02]: The monks who are not skilled, they should go and ask those monks that are skilled in calming their mind.

617
00:43:48,340 --> 00:43:49,141
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I calm my mind?

618
00:43:49,161 --> 00:43:50,061
[SPEAKER_02]: What do I attend?

619
00:43:50,081 --> 00:43:53,464
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I regard saṅkharas in order to calm my mind?

620
00:43:53,504 --> 00:43:54,524
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I calm my mind?

621
00:43:55,325 --> 00:43:57,547
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, oh, yeah, you just feel one with the universe.

622
00:43:58,507 --> 00:44:00,008
[SPEAKER_02]: Accidentally.

623
00:44:00,028 --> 00:44:01,950
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you know exactly what calmness is.

624
00:44:01,970 --> 00:44:03,891
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you start cultivating those signs.

625
00:44:05,012 --> 00:44:08,154
[SPEAKER_02]: By not acting, by not giving in, by attending this, attending... Why?

626
00:44:08,254 --> 00:44:10,576
[SPEAKER_02]: By knowing why you want to do before you do it.

627
00:44:13,322 --> 00:44:14,503
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I attend saṅkharas?

628
00:44:14,944 --> 00:44:18,649
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I attend the determinate containers for my attention, for my action?

629
00:44:18,909 --> 00:44:22,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Tell me how to regard them, so I'm not pulled by my attention, by my action.

630
00:44:24,536 --> 00:44:25,678
[SPEAKER_01]: How to regard them?

631
00:44:25,878 --> 00:44:26,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

632
00:44:27,300 --> 00:44:29,042
[SPEAKER_02]: How to maintain the context in regard to them?

633
00:44:29,983 --> 00:44:31,325
[SPEAKER_00]: How to not lose the sight of them?

634
00:44:34,353 --> 00:44:36,735
[SPEAKER_02]: Why, why, as I said before, why would you be acting out of...

635
00:44:37,736 --> 00:44:38,997
[SPEAKER_02]: I need to attend every thought?

636
00:44:39,438 --> 00:44:42,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the context of it, not attending, is unpleasant.

637
00:44:43,882 --> 00:44:48,766
[SPEAKER_02]: And you obviously lost the sight of it, because now you're trying to control it through attending and investigating every second of it.

638
00:44:49,647 --> 00:44:51,749
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's all rooted in the same context that's still there.

639
00:44:52,169 --> 00:44:54,631
[SPEAKER_02]: And that context is the saṅkhara of all of your actions.

640
00:44:55,032 --> 00:44:57,914
[SPEAKER_02]: It determines the direction of every single action you do on account of it.

641
00:44:58,755 --> 00:44:59,596
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how do I then

642
00:45:02,227 --> 00:45:05,029
[SPEAKER_02]: Regain the right perspective on those saṅkharas.

643
00:45:05,169 --> 00:45:06,110
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I regard them?

644
00:45:06,330 --> 00:45:10,914
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I won't be pulled by things that I'm attending to, or needing to, or pressured by those things.

645
00:45:11,695 --> 00:45:21,823
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, it seems like the most kind of straightforward to the point one is like, like, discerning how you, as we said before, how do I feel about the... this, like, intention that I'm doing?

646
00:45:21,843 --> 00:45:24,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I doing it because I really, like, feel a need to do it?

647
00:45:25,097 --> 00:45:31,583
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, clarifying motivation behind your actions is already the beginning of samatha, because it's already the beginning of unification.

648
00:45:32,504 --> 00:45:43,874
[SPEAKER_02]: See, like in day-to-day life, reasons for doing something is the un... mind is unified to that extent throughout all the actions of doing that thing.

649
00:45:45,255 --> 00:45:50,660
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to, I don't know, drink some tea or something.

650
00:45:54,286 --> 00:46:04,003
[SPEAKER_02]: All the little things you do, preparing tea, getting together, boiling the water, drinking it, it's already unified by the intention to have tea being drunk.

651
00:46:06,728 --> 00:46:07,690
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's usually

652
00:46:09,377 --> 00:46:12,259
[SPEAKER_02]: Things that we attend to, pull us away from what we already know.

653
00:46:12,319 --> 00:46:13,600
[SPEAKER_02]: You already know you want to have tea.

654
00:46:13,800 --> 00:46:14,580
[SPEAKER_02]: You already know what tea.

655
00:46:14,600 --> 00:46:15,341
[SPEAKER_02]: You already know everything.

656
00:46:15,641 --> 00:46:17,342
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to go and...

657
00:46:17,402 --> 00:46:18,202
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how... what do you do?

658
00:46:18,222 --> 00:46:23,826
[SPEAKER_02]: I need to take the cup, take the cup, put the bag, put the bag... No.

659
00:46:23,926 --> 00:46:33,632
[SPEAKER_02]: You just do all of that, because it's already unified by the intention to have the tea extracted, basically, from the bag, with the hot water, and you already know how to do it.

660
00:46:35,617 --> 00:46:40,139
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the context is already there, and to that extent, your mind is unified with that purpose.

661
00:46:40,519 --> 00:46:42,460
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now, find even more general purpose.

662
00:46:43,340 --> 00:46:45,521
[SPEAKER_02]: Purpose of not acting out of craving.

663
00:46:46,081 --> 00:46:47,021
[SPEAKER_02]: Make that your context.

664
00:46:48,602 --> 00:46:52,723
[SPEAKER_02]: And that becomes your unified mind upon never acting out of craving in regards to whatever.

665
00:46:53,124 --> 00:46:54,564
[SPEAKER_02]: Tea or other things, doesn't matter.

666
00:46:55,484 --> 00:46:57,365
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's rooted in craving, it's not acted out of.

667
00:46:57,385 --> 00:47:02,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you clarified that context to the point of actually, yeah, now your mind is unified in it.

668
00:47:05,081 --> 00:47:06,362
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's already samādhi.

669
00:47:07,583 --> 00:47:07,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

670
00:47:07,843 --> 00:47:16,090
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to the typical thing would be like keep asking yourself why you want to tea while you're doing everything and just distract yourself in the fact that there is something... That's already there.

671
00:47:16,110 --> 00:47:17,291
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason you can... Why do I want...

672
00:47:17,311 --> 00:47:18,472
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because it was already there.

673
00:47:18,492 --> 00:47:19,653
[SPEAKER_02]: You already know what it is.

674
00:47:19,713 --> 00:47:20,194
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what I mean.

675
00:47:20,214 --> 00:47:24,837
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on that level of discerning what's already there without you needing to attend to it.

676
00:47:26,681 --> 00:47:38,726
[SPEAKER_02]: Your body postures, or the body as a whole... And, obviously, the more... the more effort you invest in it, it becomes even, like, even more specific things, then, you can be aware of peripherally, without attending to them.

677
00:47:41,447 --> 00:47:45,048
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, you know, in the beginning, specific things, you know, I must focus, I must focus on this.

678
00:47:46,449 --> 00:47:49,790
[SPEAKER_02]: But the practice of samādhi is opposite direction of practice of focusing.

679
00:47:51,173 --> 00:47:58,878
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not unifying things through, like, absorbing in one point, but it's unifying things through finding one point that unifies everything else within it.

680
00:48:04,502 --> 00:48:05,923
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, it is one-pointedness.

681
00:48:06,563 --> 00:48:14,008
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just one... such a vast point, vast container, that whatever occurs within it is seen against that perspective.

682
00:48:14,809 --> 00:48:16,330
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's a very unified mind.

683
00:48:19,615 --> 00:48:34,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, overall, then, in that kind of context are the work to be done when there are problems and there are states of craving or craving just arising, not the state, but the craving, is eh...

684
00:48:35,407 --> 00:48:39,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining the context within which craving is.

685
00:48:39,570 --> 00:48:47,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, even if you kind of have gone into the craving, you know, it's not going to start perfect, but you just still have that kind of modus operandus about it.

686
00:48:47,557 --> 00:48:47,837
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

687
00:48:48,494 --> 00:48:50,436
[SPEAKER_02]: You include that in that context.

688
00:48:50,456 --> 00:48:58,322
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, it doesn't matter how much you were pulled by the craving and what other things... choices out of craving you made on account of that craving.

689
00:48:59,223 --> 00:49:00,844
[SPEAKER_02]: It's still within how you feel it.

690
00:49:02,726 --> 00:49:07,170
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't be making choices of getting rid of the pain, if pain is not felt.

691
00:49:08,371 --> 00:49:15,957
[SPEAKER_02]: So, not losing the context of your feeling, otherwise, means being properly mindful of your feelings, regardless of the craving of things done out of craving.

692
00:49:16,335 --> 00:49:19,457
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why that sense restraint and virtue needs to be developed beforehand.

693
00:49:20,899 --> 00:49:33,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Because when that has become your... when your behavior has become behavior of non-physical engagement with sensuality, ill-will and so on, that's how you already form the context.

694
00:49:34,749 --> 00:49:36,671
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know what I do, but I know what I will not do.

695
00:49:37,451 --> 00:49:39,092
[SPEAKER_02]: That's already a threshold for your start.

696
00:49:39,953 --> 00:49:41,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, within that, I know I will not do that.

697
00:49:42,335 --> 00:49:44,597
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me invest more effort in discerning what I'm doing

698
00:49:45,484 --> 00:49:49,767
[SPEAKER_02]: Within that broader non-doing of non-physically engaging with greed, aversion, delusion.

699
00:49:50,308 --> 00:49:51,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, but I am verbal.

700
00:49:51,509 --> 00:49:52,729
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so let me not do that.

701
00:49:53,850 --> 00:49:54,651
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, it comes the point...

702
00:49:54,671 --> 00:49:57,413
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so I'm mentally... And mentally, you engage with it when I said...

703
00:49:58,173 --> 00:49:59,835
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I will read the suttas and practice dhamma.

704
00:50:00,075 --> 00:50:00,335
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

705
00:50:00,495 --> 00:50:01,776
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I want to get rid of this pain.

706
00:50:02,717 --> 00:50:03,578
[SPEAKER_02]: But not through like...

707
00:50:03,738 --> 00:50:04,178
[SPEAKER_02]: Wait a minute.

708
00:50:04,218 --> 00:50:08,361
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in order to get rid of the pain, you need to understand the pain that's here and not try to get rid of it by default.

709
00:50:09,242 --> 00:50:11,623
[SPEAKER_02]: But you'll never see that if your behavior hasn't

